::ALG2_U06_L05_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model type fits data with a constant difference? {
= linear#Correct. A constant difference means a constant rate of change.
~ exponential#Exponential models use a constant ratio or percent change.
~ quadratic#Quadratic models have changing first differences.
~ rational#Rational models often involve denominator behavior and asymptotes.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model type fits data with a constant ratio? {
~ linear#Linear models use a constant difference.
= exponential#Correct. A constant ratio indicates repeated multiplication.
~ absolute value#Absolute value models have a V-shaped graph, not a repeated ratio.
~ constant#A constant model has no changing ratio between distinct nonzero values.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model is best for a ball's height over time when it rises and falls? {
~ linear#A linear model cannot represent both rising and falling with one turning point.
~ exponential#Exponential models do not show the parabolic path of projectile height.
= quadratic#Correct. Projectile height is commonly modeled by a quadratic function.
~ rational#A rational model is not the usual model for projectile height.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model is best when a cost starts at 50 dollars and increases by 12 dollars per item? {
~ y=50(1.12)<sup>x</sup>#This models percent growth, not fixed dollar increase.
~ y=12(50)<sup>x</sup>#This reverses the starting value and creates exponential behavior.
~ y=50/x+12#This is rational and does not match fixed added cost.
= y=12x+50#Correct. The slope is 12 and the initial cost is 50.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model is best when a population starts at 500 and grows 3% per year? {
= P=500(1.03)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. The model has initial value 500 and 3% growth.
~ P=500+3t#This adds 3 units per year, not 3%.
~ P=3(500)<sup>t</sup>#This reverses the starting value and growth information.
~ P=500(0.97)<sup>t</sup>#This models 3% decay, not growth.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Model A has residuals close to 0 with no pattern. What does that suggest? {
~ Model A should be rejected immediately.#Small random residuals usually support the model.
= Model A may be a good fit.#Correct. Small residuals without pattern suggest reasonable fit.
~ Model A has no equation.#Residuals do not determine whether an equation exists.
~ Model A must be quadratic.#Residuals alone do not force a quadratic model.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Model A has curved residuals after a linear regression. What should you consider? {
~ Keep the linear model because residuals never matter.#Residual patterns are important for judging fit.
~ Use the model with the largest prediction instead.#Largest output is not evidence of fit.
= Try a nonlinear model.#Correct. Curved residuals suggest a linear model may be missing structure.
~ Remove all data points.#Removing data without reason is not a valid model comparison step.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which comparison uses context correctly? {
~ The larger equation is better because it has more symbols.#Equation length does not prove model quality.
~ The model with the biggest prediction is always best.#A larger prediction may be unreasonable.
~ Units and constraints do not affect model choice.#Units and constraints are part of context.
= The model should match the data pattern and the real situation.#Correct. Both mathematical fit and context matter.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model has a horizontal asymptote and may fit data that levels off? {
= rational or logistic-style model#Correct. Leveling behavior often requires a model with limiting behavior.
~ linear model#Linear models keep increasing or decreasing at a constant rate.
~ simple exponential growth#Exponential growth does not level off on its own.
~ quadratic model#Quadratic models eventually rise or fall without leveling off.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>A value halves each week. Which model form matches the context? {
~ y=a-0.5x#This subtracts a fixed amount, not halves repeatedly.
= y=a(0.5)<sup>x</sup>#Correct. Halving each week is repeated multiplication by 0.5.
~ y=ax<sup>2</sup>#A quadratic model does not represent repeated halving.
~ y=a/x#A reciprocal model is not the same as halving each equal time step.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>A model must predict the number of people. Which constraint matters most? {
~ Outputs can be any negative decimal.#People cannot be negative decimals in this context.
~ Inputs must always be negative.#The context determines inputs; they are not always negative.
= Outputs should be nonnegative whole numbers when counting people.#Correct. Counts of people require nonnegative whole-number interpretations.
~ Units do not matter for people.#Units are essential for interpreting the output.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which is strongest evidence that a model is inappropriate? {
~ It uses variables.#Variables are normal in models.
~ It has a decimal coefficient.#Decimal coefficients can be meaningful.
~ It has a graph.#Graphs are useful model representations.
= It predicts negative distance in a context where distance cannot be negative.#Correct. Predictions that violate context show a model limitation.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model best matches fixed monthly savings of 75 dollars after an initial 200 dollars? {
= S=200+75m#Correct. Fixed monthly saving creates a linear model.
~ S=200(1.75)<sup>m</sup>#This models 75% growth each month.
~ S=75(200)<sup>m</sup>#This creates exponential behavior and reverses quantities.
~ S=200/m+75#This is rational and does not match fixed saving.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model best matches a car losing 15% of its value each year? {
~ V=15000-15t#This subtracts 15 dollars per year, not 15%.
= V=15000(0.85)<sup>t</sup>#Correct. Losing 15% leaves 85% each year.
~ V=15000(1.15)<sup>t</sup>#This models 15% growth.
~ V=15000/t#This rational model does not describe annual percent depreciation.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>When comparing models from a graph, which feature is most useful? {
~ Font size of labels#Formatting does not determine model fit.
~ Color of the grid#Grid color is not mathematical evidence.
= Shape and trend of the data#Correct. Shape and trend help identify model type.
~ Order of the answer choices#Answer order has no connection to model fit.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which prediction is usually more reliable? {
~ Far outside the data range#Far extrapolation is usually less reliable.
~ From a model that ignores context#Ignoring context weakens predictions.
~ From a model with patterned residuals#Patterned residuals suggest poor fit.
= Inside the data range with small residuals#Correct. Interpolation with small residuals is generally more reliable.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which residual list shows the better fit? {
= -1, 0, 2, -1#Correct. These residuals are close to 0.
~ -12, 8, 10, -9#These residuals are much farther from 0.
~ 25, 30, 35, 40#These are large positive errors, not close fits.
~ -20, -20, -20, -20#These show consistent underprediction by a large amount.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which question should be asked before trusting an extrapolated prediction? {
~ Is the prediction larger than all other answers?#Size alone does not make a prediction trustworthy.
= Is it reasonable to assume the pattern continues beyond the data?#Correct. Extrapolation depends on whether the pattern can continue.
~ Does the equation have at least two variables?#Variable count is not the key issue.
~ Can units be ignored?#Units should not be ignored in model interpretation.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model best matches a relationship with one turning point? {
~ linear#Linear models do not have turning points.
~ exponential#Basic exponential models do not turn around.
= quadratic#Correct. Quadratic models have one turning point.
~ constant#Constant models stay flat and do not turn.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model best matches inverse variation? {
~ y=mx+b#This is linear because it has a constant rate of change.
~ y=a(b)<sup>x</sup>#This is exponential because the input appears in the exponent.
~ y=ax<sup>2</sup>#This is quadratic because the input is squared, not divided into a constant.
= y=k/x#Correct. Inverse variation is modeled by y=k/x.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which reason best supports choosing an exponential model over a linear model? {
= The data changes by a percent each step.#Correct. Percent change is exponential behavior.
~ The data changes by a fixed amount each step.#Fixed amount change supports a linear model.
~ The data has no pattern.#No pattern does not support either model.
~ The table includes x-values.#Both model types can use x-values.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which reason best supports choosing a linear model over an exponential model? {
~ The output is multiplied by 1.08 each step.#That supports an exponential model.
= The output increases by 8 each step.#Correct. Constant differences support linear models.
~ The graph curves upward faster and faster.#That suggests exponential or another nonlinear model.
~ The context says percent growth.#Percent growth supports exponential models.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model comparison statement is strongest? {
~ Model A is better because it looks nicer.#Appearance is not mathematical evidence.
~ Model A is better because it is first.#Order does not determine fit.
= Model A is better because residuals are smaller and the context supports linear growth.#Correct. This uses both fit evidence and context.
~ Model A is better because it has more symbols.#More symbols do not prove a better model.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>Which model should be questioned for long-term population growth with limited resources? {
~ A model with units#Units help interpretation and should not be questioned for that reason.
~ A model with a clear initial value#Initial values are useful model features.
~ A model based on observed data#Observed data supports a model within range.
= An unlimited exponential growth model#Correct. Limited resources can prevent exponential growth from continuing forever.
}

::ALG2_U06_L05_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U06_L05_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.FN.2<br><br>Question:<br>What is the best final step after selecting a model? {
= Explain the model choice and prediction in context.#Correct. Model comparison should end with contextual interpretation.
~ Remove all units from the answer.#Units are needed for interpretation.
~ Choose a different model randomly.#Model choice should be based on evidence.
~ Ignore constraints after solving.#Constraints are part of deciding whether the model is reasonable.
}
